Solving
makeFunction2 compiles the right-hand side as (t, y) =&gt; number; makeFunctionOfT compiles the exact solution in t. The two steppers share a signature, so the method choice is a function reference.

ode = {
  const f = VM.expressions.makeFunction2(math, odeRhs)
  const exact = VM.expressions.makeFunctionOfT(math, odeExact)
  const y0 = VM.expressions.makeNumber(math, odeY0)
  if (f === null || exact === null || y0 === null) return null
  const solve = odeMethod === "Euler" ? VM.numerical.eulerSolve : VM.numerical.rk4Solve
  return {f, exact, y0, solve}
}

The solution



viewof odeN = Inputs.range([2, 64], {label: "steps n", step: 1, value: 8})








{
  const data = []
  if (ode !== null) {
    const sol = ode.solve(ode.f, 0, ode.y0, 2, odeN)
    const et = [], ey = []
    for (let i = 0; i &lt;= 200; i++) { const t = 2 * i / 200; et.push(t); ey.push(ode.exact(t)) }
    data.push({x: et, y: ey, name: "exact", line: {color: chartColors.muted, width: 2, dash: "dot"}})
    data.push({x: sol.ts, y: sol.ys, name: odeMethod, mode: "lines+markers", line: {color: chartColors.fn, width: 2}, marker: {size: 6, line: {color: chartColors.halo, width: 1.5}}})
  }
  const div = document.createElement("div")
  div.style.height = "360px"
  Plotly.newPlot(div, data, VM.plotting.layout({
    xaxis: {title: "t"}, yaxis: {title: "y"},
    legend: {x: 0.02, y: 0.98},
    annotations: ode ? [] : VM.plotting.emptyState("Check the inputs")
  }), VM.plotting.config())
  VM.plotting.autoResize(div)
  return div
}

The convergence plot
Run the solver at n = 4, 8, 16, …, record the error at t = 2, fit a line to (log h, log error). linearRegression returns the slope – the order – and xlo/xhi (the first and last point’s x, in the order given) for drawing the fitted segment.

convergence = {
  if (ode === null) return null
  const points = []
  for (let n = 4; n &lt;= 1024; n *= 2) {
    const sol = ode.solve(ode.f, 0, ode.y0, 2, n)
    const err = Math.abs(sol.ys[n] - ode.exact(2))
    if (err &gt; 0 && Number.isFinite(err)) points.push({x: Math.log10(2 / n), y: Math.log10(err), h: 2 / n, err})
  }
  return {points, fit: VM.numerical.linearRegression(points)}
}







{
  if (convergence === null || convergence.fit === null) return html`&lt;em&gt;Not enough data for a fit.&lt;/em&gt;`
  const {points, fit} = convergence
  const line = [{x: fit.xlo, y: fit.slope * fit.xlo + fit.intercept}, {x: fit.xhi, y: fit.slope * fit.xhi + fit.intercept}]
  return Plot.plot(VM.plotting.plotOptions({
    height: 300,
    x: {label: "log₁₀ h"}, y: {label: "log₁₀ |error at t = 2|"},
    marks: [
      Plot.line(line, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: chartColors.alt, strokeWidth: 1.5, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
      Plot.dot(points, {x: "x", y: "y", fill: chartColors.fn, r: 4, stroke: chartColors.halo}),
      Plot.text([{x: fit.xhi, y: fit.slope * fit.xhi + fit.intercept}], {x: "x", y: "y", text: () =&gt; `slope ≈ ${fit.slope.toFixed(2)}`, dx: 8, dy: -10, textAnchor: "end", fill: chartColors.ink})
    ]
  }))
}






Euler’s slope sits near 1, RK4’s near 4 – until the error floors out in floating point at the smallest steps, which drags the fitted slope down. l1Regression would shrug off those last points; that swap is one identifier.

Samples
seededRandom gives a uniform generator; gaussianRandom wraps it as a standard normal; scale and shift for the chosen mean and sd. The seed is part of the page’s state, so a shared URL (if your page syncs inputs to it) reproduces the same bars.

draws = {
  const gaussian = VM.sampling.gaussianRandom(VM.sampling.seededRandom(distSeed))
  const out = []
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; distCount; i++) out.push(distMean + distSd * gaussian())
  return out
}

The chart
The histogram range is fixed to the axis range rather than the samples’ own min/max, so the bins don’t shift as the seed changes; samples beyond it are clamped into the end bins. alpha makes the fill translucent in a way that follows the theme.


{
  const lo = -8, hi = 8
  const curve = VM.distributions.sampleCurve(x =&gt; VM.distributions.normalPdf(x, distMean, distSd * distSd), lo, hi)
  const bins = VM.distributions.histogramBins(draws, 48, {lo, hi})
  const div = document.createElement("div")
  div.style.height = "380px"
  Plotly.newPlot(div, [
    {type: "bar", x: bins.centers, y: bins.densities, name: `${distCount} draws`, width: bins.edges[1] - bins.edges[0],
      marker: {color: VM.plotting.alpha("fn", 0.35), line: {color: chartColors.fn, width: 1}}},
    {x: curve.xs, y: curve.ys, name: "normalPdf", line: {color: chartColors.alt, width: 2.5}}
  ], VM.plotting.layout({
    xaxis: {title: "x", range: [lo, hi]}, yaxis: {title: "density", rangemode: "tozero"},
    legend: {x: 0.02, y: 0.98}, bargap: 0
  }), VM.plotting.config())
  VM.plotting.autoResize(div)
  return div
}

A discrete variant
For a PMF, sample the curve at the integers and align one bin per integer: range: {lo: min − 0.5, hi: max + 0.5} with binCount = max − min + 1, which auto-ranging can’t guarantee.

viewof lambda = Inputs.range([0.5, 12], {label: "Poisson λ", step: 0.1, value: 4})







{
  // Poisson draws by inversion from the seeded uniform generator.
  const rng = VM.sampling.seededRandom(distSeed)
  const draws = []
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; distCount; i++) {
    let k = 0, p = Math.exp(-lambda), s = p
    const u = rng()
    while (u &gt; s && k &lt; 60) { k++; p *= lambda / k; s += p }
    draws.push(k)
  }
  const max = 25
  const pmf = VM.distributions.sampleCurve(k =&gt; VM.distributions.poissonPmf(k, lambda), 0, max, {discrete: true})
  const bins = VM.distributions.histogramBins(draws, max + 1, {lo: -0.5, hi: max + 0.5})
  const div = document.createElement("div")
  div.style.height = "300px"
  Plotly.newPlot(div, [
    {type: "bar", x: bins.centers, y: bins.densities, name: "draws", marker: {color: VM.plotting.alpha("fn", 0.35), line: {color: chartColors.fn, width: 1}}},
    {x: pmf.xs, y: pmf.ys, mode: "markers", name: "poissonPmf", marker: {color: chartColors.alt, size: 8, line: {color: chartColors.halo, width: 1.5}}}
  ], VM.plotting.layout({xaxis: {title: "k", range: [-0.5, max + 0.5]}, yaxis: {title: "P(K = k)", rangemode: "tozero"}, legend: {x: 0.7, y: 0.98}}), VM.plotting.config())
  VM.plotting.autoResize(div)
  return div
}

One global, many small files
Every file under src/js is an IIFE that adds one member (occasionally two or three that belong together) to window.VM:
(function attachVM(globalThis) {
  /** JSDoc … */
  const simpsonEstimate = (f, lo, hi, n) =&gt; { … }

  globalThis.VM = {...globalThis.VM, numerical: {...globalThis.VM?.numerical, simpsonEstimate}}
})(window)
The spread-merge means files can load in any order as far as attaching goes; the manifest exists for the few real load-time dependencies (every *Pmf/*Pdf reads logGamma; the discreteMath color helpers read VM.plotting; the SVG fullscreen button reuses the Plotly button’s icon). mustPrecede in src/manifest.mjs records them and src/manifest.test.js enforces them, along with “every file on disk is listed exactly once”.

