ojs-svg-block
For a chart drawn as inline SVG – Observable Plot, or a hand-built &lt;svg&gt; – which has no Plotly modebar. Put it on the same element as ojs-chart-block and nothing else: the library appends one persistent fullscreen button as a direct child of the block (outside every cell’s output, so a re-run that rebuilds the SVG doesn’t rebuild the button), pins it to the top-right corner of the figure (the union of the block’s SVGs at least 64 px wide, skipping the controls bar, the legend and button icons), and re-measures on resize, on re-runs and on fullscreenchange. The figure SVG is centered in the block and letterboxed in fullscreen by its viewBox ratio.
The VM.discreteMath page and the Observable Plot recipe use it. Hover the chart there to see the button.


plotly-box-large
width: 100%; height: 72vh; min-height: 500px (65vh / 400px at ≤ 768 px). It is the only size class shipped – there is no -small or -medium. For another height set div.style.height yourself, as the examples on this site do.

The panel
The expression field validates itself on every keystroke: makeFunction returns null for anything unparseable, and the field toggles vm-invalid to say so. The x₀ field accepts any constant expression – pi/2, sqrt(2) – through makeNumber.


viewof fxText = {
  const view = Inputs.text({label: "f(x)", value: "x^3 - 3x", placeholder: "an expression in x"})
  view.classList.add("ojs-fill")
  const input = view.querySelector("input")
  const check = () =&gt; input.classList.toggle("vm-invalid", VM.expressions.makeFunction(math, input.value) === null)
  view.addEventListener("input", check)
  check()
  return view
}







